course: everyday
lesson: 322
title: Home safety: blenders, loose sleeves, frozen metal, travel plugs, balconies, toddlers, medicines, fire and fireworks
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Many dangers at home are quiet ones: a blender that looks stopped can start again if it is still plugged in, and a loose sleeve can catch fire over a gas flame.
explain: Cold can stick: wet skin freezes to very cold metal or a lolly straight from the freezer, and it tears when you pull away, so let frozen things warm a little and never put your tongue on frozen metal.
explain: Plugs are different in different countries: three square pins in the UK and Ireland, two flat pins in the USA and Canada, slanted pins in Australia and New Zealand. On holiday use a proper travel adaptor and never force a plug.
explain: Medicine is never candy: children's medicines have special doses, adult medicines can harm children, and a grown-up must always give them. In a fire, leave everything, even your tablet or your pet, get out and tell the firefighters about the pet.
explain: Example: a boy in Manchester hears that his toddler sister has gone very quiet in the next room. He goes to look and finds her with a bottle of shampoo, which he gives to his mum.
fact: Before touching anything inside a blocked blender, switch it off and unplug it.
fact: Never put your hand or a spoon into a blender while it is running; the blades are very sharp.
fact: Roll up long sleeves and tie back long hair before cooking, because they can catch fire or drag a pan.
fact: Wet skin can freeze and stick to very cold metal or ice, and it tears when you pull away.
fact: The UK and Ireland use three square pins, the USA and Canada two flat pins, and Australia and New Zealand slanted pins.
fact: Forcing a plug into the wrong socket can damage it and cause an electric shock; use a proper travel adaptor.
fact: Never throw or drop anything from a balcony; even a small object falling from high up can badly hurt someone.
fact: A toddler who has gone quiet is often getting into something dangerous, so go and check.
fact: Medicine should be called medicine, not sweets or candy, so children do not go looking for more.
fact: Only a grown-up should give medicine; children's medicines have special doses, and adult medicines can harm children.
fact: In a fire, leave everything behind and get out; never go back in, and tell firefighters about any pets inside.
fact: Fireworks and sparklers burn very hot: watch from a safe distance, never pick up a used firework (an adult soaks one that did not go off in water), and put finished sparklers in water.
exercise: The blender is blocked and the food will not move. What do you do first? | Switch it off and unplug it before touching anything inside | Poke the food down gently with a long spoon while the blender keeps running | Push the food down with your fingers | Shake the blender while it is still running
exercise: Why must a blocked blender be unplugged before you reach inside? | The very sharp blades can start again if it is plugged in | Unplugging it lets the motor cool so the food goes soft again | Blenders get cold when unplugged | The plug might fall in the food
exercise: You are about to stir a pot on a gas cooker and you are wearing a long loose jumper. What should you do? | Roll up your sleeves and tie back long hair | Stir quickly so your sleeves do not burn | Hold your sleeve in your other hand | Turn the flame up so the food cooks faster
exercise: Why are loose sleeves and long hair dangerous when cooking? | They can catch fire or drag a pan off the cooker | They make the food taste of cloth and leave fluff in the soup | They make the kitchen too warm | They slow down the cooking
exercise: A girl in Alaska wants to lick a frozen metal railing to see what happens. What is true? | Her wet tongue would freeze and stick to it, and tear when pulled away | Nothing at all happens, because metal cannot get cold enough to stick to anything | Her tongue would warm the metal at once | Her tongue would slide off because ice is slippery
exercise: You take an ice lolly straight out of a very cold freezer. What is sensible? | Let it warm up before licking it | Lick it at once while it is coldest | Press your tongue on it to test it | Hold it against your lips until it softens
exercise: A family from London goes on holiday to Florida. Their phone charger has three square pins. What should they do? | Use a proper travel adaptor for American sockets | Push the plug in hard, because American sockets stretch to fit | Bend the pins to fit | Cut off the plug and twist the wires in
exercise: Which plug is used in Australia and New Zealand? | One with slanted flat pins | One with three big square pins | One with two round pins only | One with two flat pins side by side
exercise: You are on a high balcony with a small ball and want to see how it falls. What do you do? | Never drop it; take it to a safe place to play | Drop it only if the street below looks completely empty | Throw it as far as you can | Drop it, because it is only a small ball
exercise: Your toddler sister has been very quiet in the next room for a while. What should you do? | Go and check what she is doing | Enjoy the quiet and leave her alone | Wait until she cries before looking | Shout to her from where you are
exercise: An uncle tells a toddler that her medicine is candy to get her to take it. Why is this a bad idea? | She may later look for more «candy» and swallow medicine | Medicine tastes worse when called candy | Candy and medicine are the same thing | Toddlers are not allowed any sweets at all, so calling it candy is a lie
exercise: You have a headache and see painkillers in the cupboard. What do you do? | Ask a grown-up, who will give the right medicine and dose | Take one of the adult tablets, since a headache is the same at any age | Take half of an adult tablet | Take two so it works faster
exercise: The smoke alarm goes off and there is a fire. Your tablet and your cat are upstairs. What do you do? | Get out at once and tell the firefighters about the cat | Run upstairs quickly to fetch the tablet and the cat before the fire spreads | Fetch only the cat, because it is alive | Hide in your room until the fire stops
exercise: On the Fourth of July, a firework lands on the grass and stops fizzing. What should you do? | Never pick it up; leave it for an adult to deal with | Pick it up carefully to see if it will still go off again | Kick it away from the people | Light it again with a match
exercise: You have finished holding a sparkler on Bonfire Night. What do you do with it? | Put it in a bucket of water | Drop it on the grass to cool | Hand it to your little brother | Put it in your pocket to keep
test: A boy reaches into a food processor to free a stuck carrot. The machine is off but still plugged in. What is true? | It could start again; he must unplug it first | It is safe because the switch is off and the blades are still | It is safe if he is quick | It is safe if he uses his left hand
test: A girl in Toronto puts on a fluffy dressing gown with wide sleeves and goes to make soup on the gas stove. What is the danger? | The sleeve could catch fire | The soup would get too thick | The dressing gown would make her too hot | There is no danger in the kitchen
test: Why is it a bad idea to touch frozen metal with your tongue on a very cold day? | The wet skin freezes to the metal and tears when you pull away | Metal tastes bad when it is frozen and makes your tongue go numb for a day | Your tongue would melt the metal | You would get a cold from the metal
test: An Irish family goes camping in Australia and finds the sockets have slanted pins. Their plugs have three square pins. What should they do? | Use a travel adaptor made for Australian sockets | Force the plug in at an angle until the pins bend to fit | Plug in only the earth pin | Use a wet cloth to make it fit
test: Children on a tenth-floor balcony drop a tiny stone «just for fun». What is true? | Falling from that height it can badly hurt someone below | It is far too small to hurt anyone, even from that height | It will slow down and float down gently | It only matters if they aim at someone
test: A mum calls a toddler's syrup «sweets» so he takes it. The next day he climbs up to the medicine shelf. What went wrong? | He thought medicine was sweets and wanted more | He was just being naughty for no reason | The syrup was too nice to stop | Toddlers can read medicine labels
test: Which is true about medicines? | Children's medicines have special doses and adult medicines can harm children | Adult medicines work better on children because they are stronger | A child can choose their own dose | Half an adult tablet is always safe for a child
test: A boy escapes a house fire and remembers his phone is inside. What should he do? | Stay outside; nothing inside is worth his life | Run back in quickly to fetch it, before the fire reaches his room | Ask a friend to fetch it | Go in through the back door instead
test: At a New Year fireworks show, which is the safest way to watch? | From a safe distance behind the line the adults set | Right up next to the fireworks, so you get the very best view of them | Holding a lit firework in your hand | Picking up used fireworks as souvenirs
explain: A boy in England dropped a small bottle from a fifth-floor balcony to see it fall; it hit a man on the pavement below and cut his head.
explain: A teenager in Australia held a lit firework in his hand; it went off too soon and he lost part of a finger.
fact: A small object dropped from high up hits hard enough to hurt or kill someone below.
fact: Fireworks cause many burns and injuries to hands and eyes every year.
test: wanna drop a water balloon off the balcony onto ppl | Never drop anything from a balcony | It's just water, it's fine | Only drop it on friends | Only from the first floor
test: firework didnt go off, gonna check it | Stay away; an adult soaks it in water later | Go and check it straight away while it's cool | Light it again | Kick it to see
test: plug doesnt fit the hotel socket, push harder? | Use the right adapter; forcing it can shock you | Push harder | Bend the pins | Use a fork to help
test: did u hear a kid picked up a used firework on bonfire night and "it went off in his hand". lesson? | Never pick up a used firework; they can still burn very hot | Used fireworks are always safe to touch once the sparks have stopped | Pick them up with one finger | Kick them away instead
test: my cousin says she forced a UK plug into an American socket and "the whole hotel blacked out". what's the true bit | Forcing a plug can cause a shock; use a proper travel adaptor | Plugs fit every socket | Forcing it is fine if you push hard and fast so it can't spark | Adaptors are only for phones
test: kids say the boy whose sleeve caught fire cooking "is a pyro" (someone who loves setting fires). pass it on? | No, that's unkind; just roll up sleeves before cooking | Yes, he's a pyro | Yes, and tell the teacher he likes fire | Yes, it's a funny story
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the plugs used in the United Kingdom and Ireland? | Three square pins | Two flat pins, side by side | Two round pins only | Two slanted flat pins
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a toddler who has become quiet should be checked. | Quiet toddlers are often getting into something dangerous | Quiet toddlers are invariably asleep, and should be woken for their meals | Silence indicates illness in every case | Toddlers must be woken regularly
exam: It is given that a fire has started in a dwelling where a pet lives. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | Everyone gets out, and the firefighters are told about the pet | A person returns inside to fetch the pet | The family remains inside with the pet | The pet is searched for before leaving
exam: State the reason for which wet skin must not touch very cold metal. | It may freeze, stick and tear when pulled away | It makes the metal warmer | It causes the metal to rust | It causes the skin to sweat
